Yes. The cleanest canonical design is to treat verification as a **stack of independent witnesses**, not as one giant ML model and not as a pile of redundant libraries.

The main correction is:

> **ML should only verify the dimensions that are genuinely semantic or probabilistic. Deterministic transformations should be proven deterministically. Existing Sanskrit tools should own morphology/segmentation. Existing factuality tools should own generic entailment-style checks. Pāṭala should own the scholarly contracts, failure classes, provenance and authority ceilings.**

## The actual verification stack

Think of every layer as producing a proof bundle from four possible evidence classes:

```text
D = deterministic proof
W = external machine witnesses
M = learned / model-based semantic evaluation
H = human / scholar adjudication
```

Not every layer needs all four.

The canonical rule should be:

[
Proof(L) = D_L \cup W_L \cup M_L \cup H_L
]

while preserving which component established what.

A Vidyut morphology agreement should never become “translation verified,” and an LLM entailment judgment should never become “scholar approved.”

---

# 1. SOURCE

### What the layer claims

> This exact Sanskrit source span is the thing all downstream objects derive from.

### Use what already exists

**Pāṭala native:**

* hashes;
* stable IDs;
* span resolution;
* source registry;
* witness/version relationships;
* supersession.

**External:**

* GROBID / Docling for document extraction where needed;
* RO-Crate/PROV-style packaging/provenance;
* OCR tooling only when source material actually requires OCR.

### ML verification?

Almost none.

This layer should be overwhelmingly deterministic:

```text
bytes/hash
span resolution
roundtrip
Unicode normalization
locator uniqueness
source mutation detection
```

Machine proof:

```text
SOURCE_PROOF
  hash_match = PASS
  span_resolution = PASS
  character_coverage = 100%
  unidentified_content = 0
```

**Do not spend Agent 1 time ML-verifying SOURCE.**

---

# 2. T1 — this is where Sanskrit ML matters

This is the first genuinely semantic transformation:

```text
SOURCE
→ transliteral word/phrase gloss
```

Here you already have a surprisingly good verification ensemble.

## Existing witness 1: Vidyut

Ambuda describes Vidyut as its “reliable infrastructure for Sanskrit software”; it is actively maintained. ([GitHub][1])

You already have a calibrated Vidyut-based morphology lane internally.

So Vidyut should remain the primary deterministic/lexicon-based machine witness for:

```text
lemma
inflection
morphological possibilities
sandhi/segmentation candidates
```

Not English meaning.

---

# 3. Existing witness 2: Sanskrit Heritage

This is also ideal.

The Heritage tooling exposes:

* morphological analysis;
* segmentation;
* sandhi;
* declensions;
* conjugations;
* parsing/semantic roles;
* lexicon access. ([GitHub][2])

You are already using Vidyut × Heritage disagreement as a calibrated morphology signal.

**Keep that. Don't rebuild morphology ML.**

Canonical T1 linguistic evidence:

```text
TOKEN
 │
 ├─ Vidyut analysis
 ├─ Heritage analysis
 │
 └─ optionally neural witness
        ↓
MachineMorphologyEvidence
```

Then record:

```text
CONSENSUS
SINGLE_WITNESS
CONFLICT
UNANALYZED
```

You already have this conceptual machinery.

---

# 4. Add ByT5-Sanskrit only as a third independent witness

ByT5-Sanskrit is relevant because it jointly handles:

* Sanskrit word segmentation;
* lemmatization;
* morphosyntactic tagging;

and was evaluated as a preprocessing model for Sanskrit NLP/translation. ([arXiv][3])

Therefore don't replace:

```text
Vidyut + Heritage
```

with:

```text
ByT5
```

Use:

```text
Vidyut       symbolic/lexical
Heritage     independent classical analyzer
ByT5         neural witness
```

Then disagreement becomes useful data.

For example:

```text
Vidyut   NOM.SG
Heritage NOM.SG
ByT5     NOM.SG

→ strong machine consensus
```

versus:

```text
Vidyut   GEN.SG
Heritage GEN.SG
ByT5     NOM.SG

→ disagreement recorded
```

Still not truth, but a far better evidence profile.

And importantly, **don't integrate ByT5 until the existing two-witness ensemble fails materially on T1-HARD**.

That's the reuse-first doctrine.

---

# 5. CharSS / newer segmentation work is benchmark material, not another dependency

CharSS reports strong Sanskrit word-segmentation results using a character-level transformer. ([arXiv][4])

Useful?

Yes.

Do we immediately integrate it?

No.

You already have:

```text
Vidyut
Heritage
possibly ByT5
```

If segmentation is empirically a major failure source, Agent 1 can benchmark:

```text
Vidyut
Heritage
ByT5
CharSS
```

on a frozen Pāṭala segmentation set.

Only adopt another system if it improves:

```text
accuracy
coverage
abstention
or review burden
```

This should be a standing rule for every external ML component.

---

# 6. T1 gloss verification itself is not morphology

This is the part you actually need Pāṭala-specific ML for.

Suppose:

```text
vimarśa
```

is morphologically obvious.

Vidyut can verify form.

Heritage can verify form.

Neither can establish whether:

```text
reflexion
reflexive awareness
discursive reflection
self-apprehension
```

is the appropriate contextual English gloss.

So T1 proof separates:

```text
FORM PROOF
   Vidyut/Heritage/neural witnesses

SEMANTIC PROOF
   held-out T1 gold
   alternative acceptable readings
   technical-sense benchmark
   ambiguity/abstention benchmark
```

That's the first real Agent-1 ML problem.

---

# 7. GlossLM belongs here — as a baseline, not verifier

GlossLM solves automatic interlinear gloss generation.

That's extremely close methodologically to T1.

But its role should be:

```text
Pāṭala Agent2 T1 generator
        VS
GlossLM
        VS
generic LLM
```

on the **same T1-HARD test**.

It doesn't certify Agent 2.

It establishes an external baseline.

That's academically valuable because you can say:

> Agent 2's method beats / does not beat an established gloss-generation model on the same frozen Sanskrit philosophical data.

That's the proper use.

